# Pinia — Modern State Management

> **Architectural rule of this app:** Pinia is the ONLY production state
> library. Vuex appears in this course solely as legacy study material.

## Define a store

```ts
export const useCounterStore = defineStore('counter', {
  state: () => ({ count: 0, history: [] as number[] }),
  getters: {
    doubled: (state) => state.count * 2,
    lastChange(state) { return state.history.at(-1) }
  },
  actions: {
    increment() {
      this.history.push(this.count)
      this.count++                       // mutate directly — no mutations concept
    },
    async load() {
      this.count = await api.fetchCount()
    }
  }
})
```

Actions mutate `this` directly. There is no mutation/action split to maintain,
and TypeScript infers everything.

## Setup-style stores (composition)

```ts
export const useCounterStore = defineStore('counter', () => {
  const count = ref(0)
  const doubled = computed(() => count.value * 2)
  function increment() { count.value++ }
  return { count, doubled, increment }   // anything returned = usable
})
```

Stores compose by calling other stores inside actions — no namespaced modules,
no rootState gymnastics.

## Testing pattern used across THIS app

```ts
beforeEach(() => setActivePinia(createPinia()))  // fresh world per test
```

See `src/stores/__tests__/progress.store.spec.ts` for the real thing.

